malized_content_hash":"sha256:ef70c8f34a45245ca75802a9e30cc41b4ed70c23e5a9df33c3e739d41ebd4ee8","order":1,"original_content_hash":"sha256:ef70c8f34a45245ca75802a9e30cc41b4ed70c23e5a9df33c3e739d41ebd4ee8","parser":{"profile_hash":"sha256:d090f9aac6bdb0f8551ee86febd1cfecbd4af2e678328ed37bbe10b426ab1526"},"physical_order":1,"raw_blob_id":"523d133a-0fe4-5bf4-b252-b52fff39c11d","raw_file_hash":"sha256:977d76df0013e7e271b1c663a91be66a66a0c84663b696ae139d76c4197f2868","source_chapter_id":"body","source_locator":{"container_path":null,"field":"text","offset_kind":"HTML_SELECTOR","original_byte_offset":null,"path":"hydraulics-17.html","record":1,"selector":".mt-content-container"},"text":"Figure \\(\\PageIndex{2}\\): Schematic representation of a flow control valve.\nA flow control valve regulates the speed of an actuator by controlling the flow rate of hydraulic fluid. The basic components of a flow control valve include:\nA valve body with inlet and outlet ports\nA tapered adjustment screw\nAn adjustment knob\nAn internal check valve poppet\nA small spring to assist the poppet function\nThis valve can be configured to restrict flow in one direction while allowing near-unrestricted flow in the other. This is accomplished using the check valve in parallel with the variable orifice. The schematic symbol for this type of valve typically shows a variable restriction with a check valve in parallel, often drawn as a parallel line bypassing the orifice.\nFigure \\(\\PageIndex{1}\\): Control valve schematic symbol and explanation.\nThe control valve schematic symbol is drawn with a needle valve inline with the transmission lines and parallel will be a check valve. A second version provides a ghost line around the device to represent that both components are in the same valve body.","title":"15.2: Operation of a Flow Control Valve and its Scematic Symbol","volume":"","work_id":"hydraulics-17","work_title":"hydraulics-17"}],"work_id":"hydraulics-17"}